{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB06723 - MONDO_7186",
  "verbalization": "Aluminum hydroxide is implicated in the pathogenesis of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) through a series of interconnected pathways. The drug is associated with the treatment of peptic esophagitis, duodenal ulcers, and gastric ulcers, all of which are conditions that can contribute to GERD. The mechanism by which aluminum hydroxide exerts its therapeutic effects is not fully understood, but it is hypothesized to involve the neutralization of gastric acid and the protection of the gastric mucosa. This is supported by its indication for duodenal ulcers and gastric ulcers, which are known to be associated with GERD. The indirect pathway through vonoprazan, a proton pump inhibitor, further suggests a role in GERD management, though the direct mechanism of action of aluminum hydroxide remains unclear. The cumulative evidence from these paths indicates that aluminum hydroxide may contribute to the management of GERD through its protective effects on the gastric mucosa, though the precise mechanism requires further investigation.",
